WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.040
Slice an embryonic mouse cortex.

2
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:08.680
Look at the cells caught in mitosis.

3
00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:14.060
Nearly all of them sit against the ventricle, the fluid-filled space at the center

4
00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:18.440
of the brain. The cells copying their DNA sit farther out.

5
00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:23.560
Why would a cell copy its DNA in one place and divide in another?

6
00:00:24.383 --> 00:00:29.443
The early wall looks layered, with nuclei stacked at many heights.

7
00:00:30.463 --> 00:00:34.223
Nineteenth-century anatomists read that as several kinds of cells.

8
00:00:35.283 --> 00:00:39.423
In 1935, Frederick Sauer argued for something simpler.

9
00:00:40.283 --> 00:00:42.163
Each cell is long and thin.

10
00:00:42.843 --> 00:00:47.283
It reaches from the apical surface, beside the ventricle, to the basal surface,

11
00:00:47.563 --> 00:00:50.343
under the pia. Only the nucleus moves.

12
00:00:51.833 --> 00:00:56.733
Picture a lift. The shaft is the cell, and it stays fixed.

13
00:00:57.573 --> 00:00:58.973
The car is the nucleus.

14
00:00:59.653 --> 00:01:03.333
It rides up and down on a timetable, the cell cycle.

15
00:01:04.493 --> 00:01:07.173
In G1, the nucleus moves basally.

16
00:01:08.373 --> 00:01:13.473
S phase, when DNA is copied, happens in the basal part of the ventricular zone.

17
00:01:14.493 --> 00:01:18.193
In G2, the nucleus moves back to the apical surface.

18
00:01:19.233 --> 00:01:20.353
Mitosis happens there.

19
00:01:21.117 --> 00:01:23.037
The movement has motors.

20
00:01:23.877 --> 00:01:30.537
In embryonic rat cortex, knocking down motor proteins showed that a kinesin, Kif1a,

21
00:01:30.817 --> 00:01:33.597
carries the nucleus basally in G1.

22
00:01:34.557 --> 00:01:37.617
Cytoplasmic dynein pulls it apically in G2.

23
00:01:38.457 --> 00:01:40.237
That split was measured in rat.

24
00:01:41.057 --> 00:01:47.237
In zebrafish retina, the apical movement has been reported to depend on actomyosin instead.

25
00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:51.970
Where a cell divides does not tell you what its daughters become.

26
00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:58.050
As neurogenesis begins, neuroepithelial cells become radial glia.

27
00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:03.450
They keep the shape, a foot on the ventricle and a long fiber to the pia.

28
00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:05.470
They keep the lift too.

29
00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:12.210
In direct neurogenesis, a radial glial cell divides into another radial glial cell

30
00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:19.470
and a neuron. In filmed rat slices, that neuron moved out along the parent's own fiber.

31
00:02:19.733 --> 00:02:22.793
So the radial glial cell stays put.

32
00:02:23.513 --> 00:02:29.533
Its nucleus copies DNA in the basal ventricular zone, rides back, and divides at the

33
00:02:29.533 --> 00:02:35.613
ventricle. In embryonic rodent cortex, the cell that guides migrating neurons is

34
00:02:35.613 --> 00:02:37.753
also the cell that makes most of them.

35
00:02:38.473 --> 00:02:43.253
Keep the rule. The cell stays put, and its nucleus rides the cell cycle.
